    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1240">

                <pb facs="0909" n="833" sameAs="frus:frus1916/pg833"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 817.812/195</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The British Ambassador to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">British Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-04-12">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 12, 1916</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In my note No. 393 of October 28
                        last,<anchor n="12" xml:id="d1240-f1"/> I had the honour, under instructions
                    from His Majesty’s Principal Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs to enter a
                        <hi rendition="#italic">caveat</hi> against the payment by the United States
                    Government to the Nicaraguan Government of the sum of three million dollars
                    provided in the American Nicaraguan Treaty without any provision being made for
                    the settlement of certain British claims recognized in a formal agreement
                    concluded in 1912 by His Majesty’s Government and the Nicaraguan Government, I
                    added that there were other claims for goods or monies supplied <pb facs="0910"
                        n="834" xml:id="pg834"/> by British subjects to the Nicaraguan Government,
                    which were not provided for in the above agreement, as they had never been
                    disputed by the Nicaraguan Government, and for the payment of some of which
                    assurances had also been given by that Government that they would be met out of
                    the first monies received by them.</p>
                <p>I have now the honour to state that a list of these claims is in the course of
                    preparation and will be communicated to you as soon as received for inclusion in
                    the settlement, which His Majesty’s Government have no doubt will be carried out
                    in such a manner as to enable the Nicaraguan Government to meet their
                    obligations.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cecil Spring Rice</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
